\wg21_head(
  title = {The discombobulation\br of extensible math functions}
){
\dl{
  \dt{Document number:} \dd{\docnum{D4461R1}}
  \dt{Date:}            \dd{\tt{2026-11-09}}
  \dt{Audience:}        \dd{SG6}
  \dt{Project:}         \dd{ISO/IEC 14882 Programming Languages — C++, ISO/IEC JTC1/SC22/WG21}
  \dt{Reply-to:}        \dd{Jan Schultke <\mail{janschultke@gmail.com}>}
  \dt{GitHub Issue:}    \dd{\ref("https://wg21.link/P4461/github")}
  \dt{Source:}          \dd{\ref("https://github.com/eisenwave/cpp-proposals/blob/main/src/ext-math-problems.cow")}
}
\hr
}

\Babstract{
\ref("P4188R1") has a clear customization mechanism,
which is the main focus of the paper.
However, low-level numerical details are treated as out-of-scope,
which makes it difficult to use and customize the proposed functions for arbitrary types.
This issue is presented using a case study of the \tcode{std::math::sqrt} customization point,
and the issue's impact on users and library authors is presented.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

\ref("P4188R1") proposes \q{extensible math functions}.
For example, there would be a new \tcode{std::math} function object
which acts as a customizable alternative to \tcode{std::sqrt},
which only supports floating-point types and also has a \tt{std::complex} overload.
While \tcode{std::math::sqrt} is somewhat clearly specific for these inputs
because it simply delegates to \tcode{std::sqrt},
for user-defined types,
few requirements are imposed.

There are significant issues with the lack of clarity
and the limited usability of the customization point,
which are described below.

\h2{The difficulty of specifying a math function}

To understand the issue,
we must first consider what aspects of a mathematical function
may need to be specified for floating-point operands.

\h3{Aspects of a math function}

Any mathematical function that involves floating-point numbers has the following aspects:

\dl{
  \dt{Mathematical result}
  \dd{The result of the mathematical operation, in terms of real number arithmetic.}

  \dt{Sign of zero}
  \dd{Whether negative or positive zero is returned.}

  \dt{Subnormal flushing}
  \dd{
    Whether subnormal numbers are treated as if they were zero.
    This can typically be configured via CPU flag and may improve performance
    because handling subnormal numbers is much more expensive.
  }

  \dt{Domain of definition}
  \dd{
    The set of input values for which the function is defined.
    Inputs outside this domain result either in NaN,
    in an unspecified result, or in undefined behavior.
    In \header{cmath} functions, a domain error may occur.
  }

  \dt{Overflow and underflow}
  \dd{
    In what cases an overly large result causes overflow (resulting in infinity)
    or underflow (resulting in a subnormal number or zero, i.e. a \dfn{tiny} result).
    Note that underflow only occurs when a result is both inexact and tiny,
    and there is a question of whether tininess is detected before or after rounding.
  }

  \dt{Infinity and NaN propagation}
  \dd{
    How the function handles infinity and NaN inputs.
    This is different from the domain of definition because \q{pre-existing}
    infinities and NaNs are usually propagated by \header{cmath} functions.
  }

  \dt{Response to signaling NaNs}
  \dd{
    How signaling NaNs are handled by the function.
    Typically, a quiet NaN is returned and a floating-point exception is raised.
  }

  \dt{\tt{errno} interaction}
  \dd{
    Whether upon encountering domain errors, overflow, etc.
    \tt{errno} is accessed.
  }

  \dt{Resulting NaN payloads and sign bits}
  \dd{
    What bit pattern a resulting NaN has.
    Note that there are many possible bit patterns that all represent the same NaN value,
    and these bit patterns can be used to indicate error information.
  }

  \dt{Canonical results}
  \dd{
    Whether the representation of the result is canonical,
    meaning it is not an alternative and \q{discouraged} (but valid)
    representation of the same value.
  }

  \dt{Erroneous result in the absence of NaN or infinity}
  \dd{
    How domain errors, division by zero, overflow, and underflow are handled
    when they cannot be communicated via NaN or infinity results.
    This may be the case because the numeric type cannot represent these error values,
    in which case the math function needs to return some arbitrary result
    or exit abnormally (exception, \tcode{std::terminate}, etc.).
  }

  \dt{Rounding mode}
  \dd{
    What rounding mode is used to compute the result.
    This is typically the current rounding mode of the floating-point environment.
  }

  \dt{Correct rounding}
  \dd{
    Whether correct rounding is guaranteed for the result.
    That is, whether the computed result is always precise down to the least significant bit.
  }

  \dt{Preferred quantum exponent}
  \dd{
    ISO/IEC 60559 decimal interchange floating-point types represent numbers
    as an integer \math{\mi{m}} and a base-10 exponent \math{\mi{e}},
    so that the mathematical values is
    \math{\mrow{\mi{m}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mi{e}}}},
    where \math{\msup{\mn{10}\mi{e}}} is the \dfn{quantum} and
    \math{\mi{e}} is the \dfn{quantum exponent}.
    For example, \math{\mn{0.5}} can be represented as
    \math{\mrow{\mn{5}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mi{-1}}}} but also as
    \math{\mrow{\mi{50}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mi{-2}}}}.
    A library can specify a \dfn{preferred quantum exponent} (often zero)
    which is chosen when possible.
  }

  \dt{Floating-point exceptions}
  \dd{
    The floating-point exceptions raised by the function, if any.
    The C standard floating-point exceptions include
    \tcode{FE_INVALID},
    \tcode{FE_DIVBYZERO},
    \tcode{FE_OVERFLOW},
    \tcode{FE_UNDERFLOW}, and
    \tcode{FE_INEXACT}.
    These exceptions are basically \q{flags} that are raised as a side product
    of floating-point operations, often with hardware support.
    It may also be possible to interrupt the CPU when these are raised.
    However, the support for these is entirely optional,
    and targets such as WebAssembly do not support them.
  }

  \dt{Constant evaluation}
  \dd{
    Whether constant evaluation is supported and how the compile-time behavior
    may differ from the runtime behavior.
    For example, a default floating-point environment is used for C++ constant expressions,
    and results may differ for \header{cmath} functions
    between constant evaluation and runtime evaluation.
  }
}

\Bnote{
The listed aspects are limited to scalar floating-point operations.
For SIMD types, complex numbers, rational numbers, arbitrary-precision types, etc.
there may be further considerations.
}

Of these aspects, the most important one is correct rounding,
as many mathematical properties only hold under correct rounding.
A few such mathematical properties are listed below,
but this list is not exhaustive.

\cowel_macro("mf"){\mrow{
  \mi{\cowel_put{0}}}
  \mo(stretchy=false){(}
  \cowel_put
  \mo(stretchy=false){)}
}

\dl{
  \dt{Monotonicity}
  \dd{
    Does \math{\mrow{\mi{x}\mo{≤}\mi{y}}} imply
    \math{\mrow{\mf("f"){\mi{x}}\mo{≤}\mf("f"){\mi{y}}}}?
  }

  \dt{Boundedness, non-negativity}
  \dd{
    Does \math{\mrow{\mf("f"){\mi{x}}\mo{≥}\mn{0}}} hold,
    or any other bounds?
    If the result is merely said to be an approximation within some distance
    of the mathematical result, strict bounds like non-negativity may not hold.
  }

  \dt{Fixed points}
  \dd{
    Does \math{\mrow{\mf("f"){\mi{x}}\mo{=}\mi{x}}} hold for any \math{\mi{x}}?
  }

  \dt{Multiplicativity}
  \dd{
    Does \math{\mf("f"){\mrow{\mi{x}\mi{y}}}} equal
    \math{\mrow{\mf("f"){\mi{x}}\mf("f"){\mi{y}}}}?
  }
}

\h3{Case study: square root}

Find below the table comparing the degree of detail
with which the square root operation is specified in different places.

\style{
  .sqrt-table td:nth-child(1) {
    font-weight: bold;
  }
  .sqrt-table {
    table-layout: fixed;
  }
}

\table(class="sqrt-table center striped"){
  \colgroup{
    \col(style="width: 20%")
    \col(style="width: 20%")
    \col(style="width: 20%")
    \col(style="width: 20%")
    \col(style="width: 20%")
  }
  \tr{
    \th{Detail}
    \th{\tt{std::sqrt} or \tt{std::math::sqrt(float)}}
    \th{\tt{std::sqrt}\br+ C Annex F & H}
    \th{WASM \tt{f32.sqrt}\br and \tt{f64.sqrt}}
    \th{\tt{std::math::sqrt} (user-defined types)}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Math result}
    \td{\math{\msqrt{\mi{x}}}}
    \td{\math{\msqrt{\mi{x}}}}
    \td{\math{\msqrt{\mi{x}}}}
    \td{\math{\msqrt{\mi{x}}}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Sign of zero}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
    \td{\math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mrow{\mo{±}\mn{0}}}\mo{=}\mrow{\mo{±}\mn{0}}}}}
    \td{\math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mrow{\mo{±}\mn{0}}}\mo{=}\mrow{\mo{±}\mn{0}}}}}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Subnormal flushing}
    \td{implementation-defined}
    \td{no}
    \td{no}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Domain}
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mi{x}\mo{≥}\mn{0}}}}
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mi{x}\mo{≥}\mn{0}}}}
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mi{x}\mo{≥}\mn{0}}}}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Overflow and underflow}
    \td{underflow possible}
    \td{underflow possible}
    \td{underflow possible}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Infinity/NaN propagation}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
    \td{
      \math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mi{∞}}\mo{=}\mi{∞}}},\br
      \math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mi{NaN}}\mo{=}\mi{NaN}}}
    }
    \td{
      \math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mi{∞}}\mo{=}\mi{∞}}},\br
      \math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mi{NaN}}\mo{=}\mi{NaN}}}
    }
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Signaling NaNs}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
    \td{\math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mi{sNaN}}\mo{=}\mi{qNaN}}}}
    \td{WASM only has quiet NaNs}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\tt{errno}}
    \td{\tcode{math_errhandling}}
    \td{\tcode{math_errhandling}}
    \td{not supported by WASM/emscripten}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{NaN bit patterns}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
    \td{specified since WASM 3.0 with deterministic profile}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Canonical results}
    \td(class="unspecified"){\q{typically} yes}
    \td{yes}
    \td{\tt{f32} and \tt{f64} are always canonical}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Errors when no\br{}NaN or infinity}
    \td{implementation-defined result}
    \td{impossible for\br{}ISO/IEC 60559 types}
    \td{\tt{f32} and \tt{f64} have NaN and infinity}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Rounding mode}
    \td{\tcode{FLT_ROUNDS}}
    \td{\tcode{FLT_ROUNDS}}
    \td{to nearest, ties to even}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Correct rounding}
    \td{no, not even in Annex F}
    \td{no, not even in Annex F}
    \td{yes}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Preferred quantum exponent}
    \td(class="unspecified"){no requirement}
    \td{
      \math{
        \mrow{
          \mi{Q}
          \mo(fence=true, form="prefix", stretchy=false){(}
          \msqrt{\mi{x}}
          \mo(fence=true, form="suffix", stretchy=false){)}
          \mo{=}
          \mo(fence=true, form="prefix", stretchy=true){⌊}
          \mfrac{
            \mrow{
              \mi{Q}
              \mo(fence=true, form="prefix", stretchy=false){(}
              \mi{x}
              \mo(fence=true, form="suffix", stretchy=false){)}
            }
            \mn{2}
          }
          \mo(fence=true, form="suffix", stretchy=true){⌋}
        }
      }
    }
    \td{no decimal floating-point}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Constant evaluation}
    \td{supported except for errors, may differ from runtime results}
    \td{supported except for errors, may differ from runtime results}
    \td{not applicable}
    \td(class="unspecified"){unspecified}
  }
}

Based on the properties above and using some additional guarantees provided by the specification
we can derived several secondary properties for the functions:

\table(class="sqrt-table center striped"){
  \colgroup{
    \col(style="width: 20%")
    \col(style="width: 20%")
    \col(style="width: 20%")
    \col(style="width: 20%")
    \col(style="width: 20%")
  }
  \tr{
    \th{Secondary property}
    \th{\tt{std::sqrt} or \tt{std::math::sqrt(float)}}
    \th{\tt{std::sqrt}\br+ C Annex F & H}
    \th{WASM \tt{f32.sqrt}\br and \tt{f64.sqrt}}
    \th{\tt{std::math::sqrt} (user-defined types)}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Deterministic}
    \td{❌ no, incorrect rounding, unspec. NaN payloads, compile-time and runtime differences, excess precision}
    \td{❌ no, incorrect rounding, unspec. NaN payloads, compile-time and runtime differences, excess precision}
    \td{✅ yes}
    \td{❌ no}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Monotonic}
    \td{❌ no, due to lack of correct rounding}
    \td{❌ no, due to lack of correct rounding}
    \td{✅ yes}
    \td{❌ no}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Non-negative}
    \td{✅ yes}
    \td{✅ yes}
    \td{✅ yes}
    \td{❌ no}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Fixed points}
    \td{❌ none}
    \td{
       \math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mrow{\mo{±}\mn{0}}}\mo{=}\mrow{\mo{±}\mn{0}}}}
    }
    \td{
      \math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mrow{\mo{±}\mn{0}}}\mo{=}\mrow{\mo{±}\mn{0}}}},\br
      \math{\mrow{\msqrt{\mn{1}}\mo{=}\mn{1}}}
    }
    \td{❌ none}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{Multiplicative}
    \td{❌ no}
    \td{❌ no}
    \td{⚠️ only if all operations are exact}
    \td{❌ no}
  }
}

\Bnote{
The \ref("P4188R1") column refers to guarantees provided for the customizable interface
of \tcode{std::math::sqrt},
specified by the \q{Contract} subsection in §5.1.1.1.
\ref("P4188R1") provides specializations for fundamental floating-point types
and for \tt{std::complex},
where \tcode{std::math::sqrt} is equivalent to \tcode{std::sqrt}.
However, this equivalence doesn't provide any insight into how \tcode{std::math::sqrt}
should behave for user-defined types,
including but not limited to \q{software floating-point types} (aka. \q{soft floats}).
}

\Bnote{
Some of these properties combine into other meaningful guarantees.
For example, monotonicity and the fixed points at \math{\mn{0}} and \math{\mn{1}} imply that:
\mathblock{\mrow{
  \mn{0}\mo{≤}\msqrt{\mi{x}}\mo{≤}\mn{1}
  \mo{,}\mspace(width="0.5em"){}\mtext{for}\mspace(width="0.25em"){}
  \mn{0}\mo{≤}\mi{x}\mo{≤}\mn{1}
}}

}

\h4{Too unclear for many users}

From this table,
we can tell that in generic code where we don't know the input type,
virtually all aspects are unspecified.
Without any guarantee regarding determinism, monotonicity, non-negativity, etc.,
the function becomes essentially unusable for some applications
because violating certain assumptions results in numerical instability, crashes,
and other unexpected behavior.

\Bex{
A quaternion representing a rotation should satisfy
\math{\mrow{
  \msup{\mi{w}\mn{2}}\mo{+}
  \msup{\mi{x}\mn{2}}\mo{+}
  \msup{\mi{y}\mn{2}}\mo{+}
  \msup{\mi{z}\mn{2}}
  \mo{=}\mn{1}
}}.
A function to normalize a quaternion may looks as follows:

\cppblock{
struct quat {
  number w, x, y, z;
};

quat normalize(quat q) {
  number r = std::math::sqrt(q.w*q.w + q.x*q.x + q.y*q.y + q.z*q.z);
  return {
    q.w / r, q.x / r,
    q.y / r, q.z / r,
  };
}
}

\ul{
  \li{
    If subnormal flushing or simply low accuracy can incorrectly produce zero,
    \tcode{normalize} divides by zero.
  }
  \li{
    Without a strict guarantee that \tcode{std::math::sqrt(x) >= 0} holds,
    we might get a \q{negative unit quaternion}.
  }
  \li{
    Without a deterministic \tcode{std::math::sqrt},
    we cannot guarantee that \tcode{normalize} is deterministic either.
  }
  \li{
    Without guarantees that negative inputs are accepted in \tcode{std::math::sqrt}
    (and result in NaN),
    \tcode{normalize} may crash or throw.
  }
  \li{
    Without correct rounding and with no fixed points,
    normalization could actually make the quaternion drift away from being a unit quaternion.
    Without a fixed point at \tcode{std::math::sqrt(1)},
    even exact unit quaternions may drift.
  }
}
}

While the interface is more rigorously specified for fundamental floating-point types,
those are already covered by \tcode{std::sqrt}.
A user who knows they are working with a fundamental floating-point type
has no need for this customization point and can call \tcode{std::sqrt} directly.

This leaves the use case of \tcode{std::math::sqrt} relatively limited,
to situations where we write code as if it was doing real number arithmetic,
ignoring most of these details.
To be fair, such use cases do exist, and C++26 \header{linalg} is one of them,
as it works with user-defined types and uses \tcode{operator+} as a customization point
with basically no specific requirements.

\h4{Too unclear for library authors}

This severe underspecification also makes it difficult to provide
customizations as a library author.
Consider for example how an author of Bloomberg's \tt{bdldfp_decimal} library
for decimal floating-point types would customize \tcode{std::math::sqrt}
for \tcode{bdldfp::Decimal64}:
they would have nothing more than the description \q{\math{\msqrt{\mi{x}}}},
leaving them with questions such as:
\ul{
  \li{How should rounding work?}
  \li{Should \tcode{math_errhandling} and thus \tcode{errno} be used?}
  \li{Should the preferred quantum exponent be that of \tcode{std::sqrt}?}
  \li{Should the operation strictly adhere to the ISO/IEC 60559 \tt{squareRoot} operation?}
}

\h4{Unclear design strategy}

The issues faced by users and library authors are a result of the fact
that the overall design strategy of \ref("P4188R1") is unclear.
Are these customization points intended to be
\ul{
  \li{
    \dfn{maximally strict}, i.e. adhere to ISO/IEC 60559 and perform correct rounding,
  }
  \li{
    \dfn{maximally fast}, i.e. prioritize computing \math{\msqrt{\mi{x}}}
    and deliberately ignore any of these details,
    in the spirit of \tt{-ffast-math}, or
  }
  \li{
    \dfn{maximally C-compatible}, i.e. behave as closely as possible to \tcode{std::sqrt},
    even inheriting questionable quirks like writing to \tcode{errno}?
  }
}

Since there is an unbounded set of user-defined types,
at the very least,
there needs to be a \q{fallback} or \q{when in doubt} answer whenever a question arises,
and the overall design strategy could do that.

Simply saying \q{the priority is whatever the library author wants}
does not work because the delegation of \tcode{std::math::sqrt} to \tcode{std::sqrt}
could make choices that conflict with a library author's intent.
For example, if they want to customize \tcode{std::math::sqrt} for their type to be maximally fast,
then \tcode{std::math::sqrt} would be a great inconvenience
because its performance is impeded by checking for negative inputs and writing to \tcode{errno}.
Furthermore, if one library author customizes \tcode{std::math::sqrt} to be maximally fast
and another customizes \tcode{std::math::sqrt} to be maximally strict,
how can \tcode{std::math::sqrt} be used portably in generic code by users?
They would have no idea what kind of square root they are signing up for.

\h4{Square root in \tt{<linalg>}}

The C++26 linear algebra library can be used as a case study
of how mathematical operations could be performed on user-defined types.
\eelis{linalg.reqs.alg} imposes requirements on the meaning of
\tcode{*}, \tcode{*=}, and other operators.

\ref("LWG4315") pointed out
that the specification of \tcode{vector_two_norm} and \tcode{matrix_frob_norm} is unclear
since the functions return a \q{square root} without any specification
of how a square root is computed.
This issue was resolved for C++26
by limiting those functions to floating-point types and to \tt{std::complex}.
Presumably, the \tcode{std::math::sqrt} proposed in \ref("P4188R1")
could be used to obtain such a generic square root for arbitrary types.

\h2{One or multiple customization points}

The possible strategies in \ref("#unclear-design-strategy") hint at another problem:
since there are radically different \q{top priorities} (strictness, performance, C compatibility),
it is not obvious that there should be a single \tcode{std::math::sqrt} customization point
instead of two, three, or even more customization points.
There could be \tcode{std::math::fast_sqrt} and
\tcode{std::math::cr_sqrt}
customization points, for example.

Analogously, the C standard reserves the prefix \tcode{cr_} for correctly rounded
(see \ref("#aspects-of-a-math-function"))
counterparts to the existing math functions.
Correct rounding is often difficult to implement and may incur a severe performance penalty,
which makes it a bad default for operations in general,
but it does make sense to let users explicitly opt into correct rounding.
Thus, in the future, we may have
\ul{
  \li{the current \tcode{std::sqrt} function which prioritizes performance, and}
  \li{
    a new \tcode{std::cr_sqrt} function which prioritizes strictness/accuracy,
    similar to the one in \ref("P3864R1").
  }
}

\ref("P4188R1") somewhat covers this problem in
§2.3.1.1 \q{Rounding modes, precision/performance control}
and states that there could be e.g. an additional template parameter
to provide further customization.
However, as shown in the discussion above,
it's already unclear what the \q{base form} of the customization point should be.

This problem is further compounded by the fact that different domains have different priorities.
For example, in computer graphics, accuracy is often irrelevant because
whether a vertex is off by one pixel or whether an RGB value
is off by one is usually not noticeable.
For scientific computing on the other hand,
accuracy is often paramount.
This makes a library author who is providing some numeric type
and customizing \tcode{std::math::sqrt} ask the question:
\Bquote{
Am I customizing \tcode{std::math::sqrt} for performance or for accuracy?
}
For a domain-specific third-party library,
there may be an obvious answer,
but not in general.

\h2{Conclusion}

WG21 must come to a consensus on the overarching design strategy
for the numeric behavior of extensible math functions.

\ref("P4188R1") in its current form leaves virtually all aspects of e.g. \tcode{std::math::sqrt}
up to the author's discretion,
except for fundamental types (delegated to \tcode{std::sqrt}) and for \tt{std::complex}.
Unpopular historical choices
like \tcode{std::sqrt} writing to \tcode{errno} make this delegation questionable.
\ref("P4188R1") focuses almost exclusively on the mechanism for customization;
low-level numerical details are treated as out-of-scope.

A plausible response from WG21 may also be that clarifying the behavior of the customization point
(instead of just pointing at \tcode{std::sqrt})
is effectively redesigning a math library from scratch.
It is implausible that the capacity and expertise required to do so exists in WG21
(or in SG6, the Numeric study group), as compared to WG14,
which has a larger and much more active floating-point study group.
After all, the floating-point specification in the C++ standard
has been severely neglected for decades
and only tiny extensions like \tcode{std::lerp} have been added over the years,
compared to WG14's mega-projects like new C23 math functions,
C23 Annex F and Annex H, etc.

